# A lifetime parameter does not stop a call from being a lend. A region-polymorphic callee may
# allocate into a mapped caller region — which the caller cannot observe — and it can never close
# one, because a lifetime parameter is external in the callee's own frame and no certificate may
# close an external region. What it stores through an exclusive formal is bounded exactly as on
# the summary path, which also composes a callee write without inspecting the written value. Such
# a call therefore needs no callee summary either, as long as every formal lifetime is pinned to a
# caller region that is active at the call and every actual carrying a region lands on a formal
# declared with that same lifetime.
struct Cell:
    value: mutable i64

# A region-polymorphic recursive read. No summary of this function exists while it is checked, so
# only the lend rule can admit the call.
def scan[@r](cell: Cell& @r, depth: usize) -> i64:
    requires depth <= 127
    decreases 127 - depth
    return 0 if depth >= 127
    return scan(cell, depth + 1)

# Two lifetimes at once, one lent shared and one lent exclusively. Each is pinned separately.
def merge[@r, @s](target: mutable Cell& @r, source: Cell& @s, depth: usize) -> i64:
    requires depth <= 127
    decreases 127 - depth
    return 0 if depth >= 127
    return merge(target, source, depth + 1)
